## ABSTRACT
Gas therapy (GT) exhibits great potential for clinical application due to its high therapeutic efficiency, low systemic side effects, and biosafety, thereinto, a multifunctional nanoplatform is generally needed for controllable gas release and precise delivery to tumor tissue. In this review, the recent development of multifunctional nanoplatforms for efficient tumor delivery of stimuli-responsive gas-releasing molecules (GRMs), which could be triggered by either exogenous physical or endogenous tumor microenvironment (TME) is summarized. The reported therapeutic gas molecules, including oxygen \(\left(\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)\), hydrogen sulfide \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\right)\), nitric oxide (NO), hydrogen \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)\), and carbon monoxide (CO), etc., could directly influence or change the pathological status. Additionally, abundant nanocarriers have been employed for gas delivery into cancer region, such as mesoporous silica nanoparticles (MSNs), metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), two-dimensional (2D) nanomaterials, and liposomes, as well as non-nanocarriers including inorganic and organic nanoparticles. In the end, the outlooks of current challenges of GT and GRMs delivery nanoplatforms as well as the prospects of future clinical applications are proposed.
## KEYWORDS
gas therapy (GT), gas-releasing molecules (GRMs), stimuli-responsive, exogenous, endogenous, nanoplatform
## 1 Introduction
The current treatments for cancer include surgery, radiation therapy (RT), chemotherapy, immunotherapy, starvation therapy, chemodynamic therapy (CDT), photodynamic therapy (PDT), photothermal therapy (PTT), and sonodynamic therapy (SDT), each with its own advantages and disadvantages [1]. For example, the PDT and SDT are insufficient efficacy due to the intrinsic hypoxia within tumor region, while RT and chemotherapy are prone to induce side effects to normal tissues and cancer multidrug resistance (MDR) [2–5].
Recently, gas therapy (GT) has received widespread attention, which is based on the special gas signaling molecules, including oxygen \(\left(\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)\), hydrogen sulfide \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\right)\), nitric oxide (NO), hydrogen \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)\), and carbon monoxide (CO) [1, 6]. These gas signal molecules have been proved as a messenger in the process of specific binding with multivalent transition metals, offering a variety of physiological functions to regulate almost all human systems, such as the nervous system, cardiovascular system, immune system, and respiratory system, and have a great impact on human physiological processes [7–9]. Moreover, they also own many distinctive anti-cancer properties, such as accelerating the apoptosis of cancer cells, preventing the proliferation of cancer cells by rapidly depleting the high-level bioenergy of cancer cells (i.e., Warburg effect), maintaining the energy level of normal cells, and selective protection of the activity and physiological functions for normal cells, etc. [10, 11]. These excellent performances make the GT become a peculiar treatment with low toxicity and high efficiency in the anticancer application, exhibiting superior functions to commonly used anticancer drugs currently.
for normal cells, etc. [10, 11]. These excellent performances make the GT become a peculiar treatment with low toxicity and high efficiency in the anticancer application, exhibiting superior functions to commonly used anticancer drugs currently.
Generally, the antitumor effect of these gaseous molecules exhibits the concentration-dependent with varied anti-cancer mechanisms. For example, \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) can relieve tumor tissue hypoxia, thus reduce the expression of hypoxia inducible factor-1α (HIF-1α), blocking tumor angiogenesis and metastasis [12, 13]. \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), NO, and CO gases were reported to inhibit the mitochondrial respiratory metabolism of cancer cells by solidly binding to haem iron centres of many proteins at the concentration of nM level, while NO gas can selectively reduce the expression of P-glycoprotein (P-gP), reverse cancer cell MDR and enhance chemotherapy [7, 8, 14]. In addition, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) gas can kill cancer cells by inducing oxidation stress and reactive oxygen species (ROS) production. More importantly, these gaseous molecules combining with traditional therapies could enhance the anticancer effect and reduce the side effect of non-specific toxic therapeutics to normal tissues.
However, most gases are poorly soluble in the bloodstream, highly blood toxicity, and have strong irritants to the respiratory tissue, which limits systemic administration. Moreover, the therapeutic effects of these gases depend on their concentration, diffusion velocity, location, and retention in the tumor lesions
[1, 6]. Thereinto, the gas-releasing molecules (GRMs), which can response to internal or external stimuli and release gaseous molecules in a controlled and targeted manner thus presenting less systemically toxic, have attracted much interests [15, 16]. There are two main kinds of responsive GRMs, one can be activated by the endogenous tumor microenvironment (TME), e.g., hydrogen peroxide \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)\), glutathione (GSH), weak acid (pH 6.5–6.8), or special active protease in TME [17–19], another can spatiotemporally respond to exogenous stimuli-responsive, such as light, ultrasound (US), X-rays, and magnetic field, etc. [20]. Based on the encouraging previous findings, GT emerges as a "green" tumor treatment modality that possesses following specific advantages: (i) GRMs cannot induce MDR and show low or no cytotoxic to normal tissues. (ii) GRMs can be specifically released at tumor sites for precision cancer treatment [14]. (iii) GRMs show an excellent permeability to penetrate biomembranes with rapid diffusion capability to the tumor tissues even cells.
However, the GRMs are also fraught with some limitations like inherent toxic functional groups, premature gas release, and rapid systemic clearance due to their low molecular weights, which further limit their clinical applications. To overcome these limitations, various multifunctional organic/inorganic nanocarriers with good biocompatibility have been developed as gas nanogenerators for tumor-targeted delivery of GRMs [1, 21]. Furthermore, the direct donor of gas molecules by polymer self-assembled/organic/inorganic nanoparticles without using any of nanocarriers also has been developed to employ for the TME stimuli-responsive GRMs [22, 23]. In the following chapters, we overview the recent studies on the availability of nanocarriers/nonnanocarriers for efficient tumor delivery of GRMs, and outlook the current opportunities and challenges of stimuli-responsive GRMs delivery multifunctional nanoplatforms, as well as the prospected future applications (Fig. 1).
## 2 GRMs delivery nanoplatforms based on nanocarriers
Controlled release and efficient tumor-targeted delivery of GRMs are the prerequisites to ensure effective GT. The recent developments of multifunctional organic/inorganic nanocarriers, such as mesoporous silica nanoparticles (MSNs), metal-organic
frameworks (MOFs), carbon nanomaterials, and liposomes, have been employed as gas delivery nanoplatforms for stimuliresponsive GRMs. In this section, main progresses on these multifunctional organic/inorganic nanocarriers are overviewed as follows, which are also summarized in the Table 1.
## 2.1 MSN-based gas delivery nanoplatforms
The first report of mesoporous materials was the Si/Si-Al series mesoporous material of M41S in 1992, and subsequent researches related to mesoporous materials have always been the hotspot in the scientific community [24]. As a representative of mesoporous materials, MSNs have been gained widespread attention due to their uniform and tunable mesoporous pore size, stable framework structure, large specific surface area, easy surface modification, and good biocompatibility [25–27]. Moreover, benefiting to the unique pore structure, MSN was always used as a chemotherapeutic drug carrier for stimulating responsive drug release and targeting tumor drug delivery. Moreover, many gas donor molecules could be loaded into the MSN carrier that could be triggered stimuli-responsive gas release by endogenous/exogenous-triggered systems to achieve targeted tumor effective gas therapy [26].
## 2.1.1 Exogenous-responsive of MSN-based nanocarriers
Among all the exogenous stimulus, light is the most convenient mode, which is regarded to be a common route of controllable gas delivery. However, most light-responsive GRMs prodrugs are only sensitive to ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) light and UV–vis light are always prone to induce phototoxicity due to its low tissue penetration depth, which severely limits the application of light-responsive gas release in vivo [28–30]. In comparison, near-infrared (NIR) light with the range of \(650{-}1,\!100\ \mathrm{nm}\), possesses an excellent tissue penetration depth and lower phototoxicity that own the broader application prospects in light-responsive GRMs [31, 32]. Guo et al. reported phototriggered NO nanogenerators \(\left(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{MSN-SNO-DOX}\right)\), which could promote stimuliresponsive NO release from the breakdown of S–NO bond by the irradiation of NIR, were beneficial to reverse tumor multidrug resistance and further enhance chemotherapy against MCF-7/ADR cancer (Fig. 2(a)) [33]. Subsequently, the modification of transferrin (Tf) on the surface of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{MSN-SNO-DOX}\), endows them with the capability of tumor targeting. In addition, a dose-controllable NO release nanoplatform was constructed based on MSN-coated upconversion nanoparticle (UCNP) \(\left(\mathrm{NaYbF}_{4}\mathrm{:Tm@NaYF}_{4}\mathrm{:Yb/Er}\right)\) that encapsulated with \(\left[\mathrm{NH}_{4}\right]\left[\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{3}\left(\mathrm{NO}\right)_{7}\right]\) (RBS, as NO donor) and doxorubicin (DOX) (RBS/DOX-UCNP@MSN) [34]. The mesoporous shell can efficiently adsorb RBS onto UCNPs, while the white upconversion emission leads to the maximum spectral overlap with the RBS absorbance, and then induced to generate NO under \(980\ \mathrm{nm}\) laser irradiation [34]. More interesting, the controllable dose NO release can be achieved by controlling different laser power densities that higher laser irradiation can generate highly concentration of NO to induce tumor cells apoptosis, while a low concentration of NO can significantly inhibit the multi-drug resistant protein expression (e.g., P-gP) to further enhance the synergistic therapeutic effect with chemotherapy (Fig. 2(b)). The generated NO could be detected and quantified by a free radical analyzer (TBR 1025) with a NO-sensitized electrode.
Compared with light, US can more easily focus on the local small area of human tissue and has higher tissue penetration depth (1 MHz, 20 cm), showing a unique advantage of responsive gas release [35]. As one of MSNs, hollow mesoporous organosilica nanoparticles (MONs), which possess the hollow structure and easy chemical modification, were also widely used for exogenous
stimuli-responsive drug release and targeted drug delivery. For example, a kind of fluorocarbon (FC)-chain-functionalized MONs (FHMONs) that combine the structural and functional advantages of HMONs was employed as the oxygen reservoir and IR780 sonosensitizer carrier \(\mathrm{(IR780@O_{2}{\mathrm{-FHMON}})}\) , respectively [36]. US triggers the responsive oxygen release of \(\mathrm{IR}780@{\mathrm{O}}_{2}\) -FHMON, thus relieves solid tumor hypoxia, enhancing SDT efficiency against PANC-1 cells. Furthermore, it is found that US can trigger the N,N'-di-sec-butyl-N,N'-dinitroso-1,4-phenylenediamine (BNN6, NO donor) to generate NO gas, which can combine with
superparamagnetic iron oxide nanoparticles (SPION) to guide magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) for US-responsive NO gas therapy (BNN6-SPION@hMSN) [37]. Interestingly, according to the theory of sonochemistry, the free radical inducing effect of ultrasound mainly arises from the generation of transient and superhigh temperature and pressure (thousands of Kelvin and Bar) during ultrasonic cavitation as well as sonoluminescence [37]. Meanwhile, the sonoluminescence could induce the BNN6 to generate two nitrosyl groups, named as NO free radicals as well as NO gas molecules (Fig. 2(c)). Our group previously constructed a
hollow MSN with the modification of targeting peptides and L-arginine (L-Arg, NO donor) named as Peptide-HMSN-LA [38]. Under the irradiation of US, the targeted Peptide-HMSN-LA could achieve tumor tissue deep penetration and accelerate the reaction between L-arginine and tumor tissue overexpressed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to release NO gas. Moreover, local US can also activate the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to generate ROS via the energy transformation from US mechanical energy to chemical energy, enhancing synergistic NO gas therapy against PANO-1 tumor [38].
As a non-invasive exogenous stimulus, X-rays own an extremely high penetration depth in vivo, and low-dose X-ray radiation that can potentially trigger the release of drugs both temporally and spatially without any additives [39, 40]. Compared with other exogenous stimulus, X-ray has the following advantages in controlling drug release: (i) The highly penetrating X-ray can force the drug release exclusively in a deep-seated lesion area [41]. (ii) The on-demand drug release can be achieved by varying the dose and duration of X-ray radiation [42]. (iii) The X-ray controlled drug release combining with RT can achieve to enhance the treatment efficacy [43]. Appropriate concentration of NO is the premise of sensitizing RT and realizing effective cancer therapy. Therefore, Fan et al. developed an intelligent X-ray-controlled NO-releasing upconversion theranostic system (PEG-USMSs-SNO) by engineering UCNPs with S-nitrosothiol (R-SNO)-grafted MSN [40]. Based on S–N bond cleavage upon X-ray irradiation, X-ray triggered NO release can be achieved without depth dependence, which overcomes two major barriers i.e., endogenous difference and limited penetration. Furthermore, the nanotheranostics (PEG-USMSs-SNO) not only employed for simultaneous upconversion luminescent (UCL) imaging, but also
achieved NO-sensitized RT of hypoxic tumor (Fig. 2(d)) [40]. In addition, Dou and co-workers loaded SNO and indocyanine green (ICG, a photosensitizer) onto MSN shells of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\)-doped \(\mathrm{NaGdF_{4}}\) scintillating nanocrystals to realize X-ray induced NO release and synergistic with PDT for cancer effective therapy [44].
## 2.1.2 Endogenous-responsive of MSN-based nanocarriers
The level of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in tumor cells is revealed about 3- to 26-fold than that in normal cells, which inspires researchers to design a nanomedicine of cellular endogenous \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) responsive release. \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\)-responsive gas release mainly employs the intratumoral \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to trigger nano-drugs release therapeutic gas in situ killing cancer. Our group previously developed thioether-bridged dendritic MONs as carriers to immobilize catalase (CAT), obtaining a hybrid catalytic nanoreactor (catalase@MONs) for tumor-sensitive US-high intensity focused ultrasound (HIFU) surgery owing to its controllable synthesis and potential biodegradability (Fig. 2(e)). Upon HIFU irradiation, the loaded catalase could catalyze tumor tissue overexpressed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and in situ generate \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), which not only acted as US imaging contrast agents, but also enhanced HIFU-mediated cancer therapy [45]. In addition, L-Arg and glucose oxidase (GOx) could be co-loaded into HMON to construct a glucose-\(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) cascade responsive NO responsive nanoplatform (L-Arg-HMON-GOx) [46]. The GOx could oxidized intratumoral glucose into \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and subsequent reacted with L-Arg to generate NO, achieving the synergistic tumor starving-like/gas therapy (Fig. 2(f)). Similarly, Jin et al. demonstrated that the intracellular CO2 gas release could enhance the therapeutic activity by a \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\)-responsive prodrug of flaviganese carbonyl (MnCO) [47]. Within tumor
microenvironment, the overexpressed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) could preferentially decompose MnCO into hydroxyl radicals (·OH) and CO via Fenton-like reaction. The released CO could be detected by spectrophotometrically measuring the conversion of hemoglobin (Hb) to carboxyhemoglobin (HbCO) [47].
In addition, the slightly acidic environment of tumor and inflammatory tissues also provides endogenous power for acid responsive controllable gas release [48–50]. For example, Kim et al. reported a \(\mathrm{pH}\) responsive gatekeeper for NO delivery (pH@MSN-CaP-NO) [51]. CaP coating can protect the gas prodrug from acid responsive dissolution. In the weak acidic environment, accompanying with the dissolving of CaP coating, the loaded NO prodrug 2-nitrobenzaldehyde (o-NBA) is exposed and decomposed to generate NO gas [51]. Moreover, an intelligent nanomedicine was developed by successively encapsulating iron carbonyl (FeCO) and \(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}\) into MSN (FeCO\(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}@\mathrm{MSN}\)) to realize the intratumoral acid-triggered sequential release of CO and ROS (Fig. 2(g)) [52]. The co-released CO and ROS can damage the mitochondria and DNA in tumor cells, respectively. The damaged mitochondria would cut off the energy source of ATP, inhibiting DNA self-repair, exhibiting the synergetic antitumor efficacy of GT and CDT [52]. Furthermore, Yang and co-workers constructed an ammonia borane loaded MSN (AB@MSN) to obtain a high payload delivery nanoplatform, that can be activated by acid and in situ release \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) [53]. They found that as a \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) prodrug, AB not only possessed the high
capability of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage (10 wt. \(\%\) ), but also were triggered by acid to produce \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) that was monitored by a hydrogen detector (Microsensor Monometer, Unisense Co.), and realized precision high efficacy GT [53].
## 2.2 MOF-based gas delivery nanoplatforms
MOFs is a novel class of crystalline porous materials that formed by the connection of metal ions or metal clusters with organic ligands, which was firstly reported by Yaghi et al. in 1995 [54, 55]. MOF-materials possess the potential application in catalysis, gas adsorption and separation, optoelectronic material and organic synthesis, and nano-biomedicine, due to the characteristics of large specific surface area, regular and uniform pore structure, adjustable pore size and chemical surfaces, and good biocompatibility, etc. [56–59]. Interestingly, the prodrug molecules could be easily loaded on the surface of MOFs via electrostatic adsorption, coordination, and \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking [60]. Meanwhile, the prodrug molecules can diffuse into the pore and effectively stored due to the interaction with the linkers or metal clusters when the size of prodrug molecules is smaller than the pores. Importantly, the specific groups of drug molecules can effectively form more stable covalent/dynamic bonds with functional linkers to avoid premature drug leakage [60]. Additionally, the larger size of cargoes such as nucleic acids, proteins, and nanoparticles can be confined in the framework with enhancing stability and
improving tailorability [60].
Briefly, compared with traditional nanocarriers for GRMs delivery, MOF-based nanoplatforms possess the following advantages: (i) The ultra-high specific surface area and porosity are conducive to highly efficient loading/encapsulation of GRMs, which is beneficial to the subsequent delivery and release. (ii) The coordinatively unsaturated metal sites can load GRMs via forming the metal-gas complexes by the coordination effect and the metal-gas complexes can also be introduced into organic linkers of MOF by functional modification methods [61–63]. (iii) The excellent biodegradability of MOFs ensures the on-demand releasing of therapeutic gas molecules in the tumor microenvironment [64].
## 2.2.1 Exogenous-responsive of MOF-based nanocarriers
The GRMs can spatiotemporally respond to exogenous stimuli responsive, such as light, US, and X-rays. It is found that light stimuli-responsive MOF-based nanocarriers exhibit excellent advantages against cancer due to the noninvasiveness and spatiotemporal accuracy of light [58]. Gao et al. employed UiO-66 as \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) carrier to store \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and modified ICG on the surface of UiO-66 by coordination reaction of sulfonic acid group and \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters, and then coated RBC membrane to construct an \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) self-supply PDT nanoplatform \((\mathrm{O}_{2}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66@\mathrm{ICG}@\mathrm{RBC})\) [65]. Herein, \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) could be determined by a portable dissolved oxygen meter (Rex, JPBJ-608, China), which was efficiently released from this nanoplatform due to the thermal effect of ICG by NIR light irradiation and the generation of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) could further significantly enhance the PDT effect on MCF-7 tumor (Fig. 3(a)). Moreover, the capsulated RBC membrane shows the capability of improving long circulation of nanoparticle and enhancing the enhanced permeability and retention (EPR) effect in tumor treatment. Yao and co-workers reported a DOX loaded Mn carbonyl modified Fe(III)-based MOFs (MIL-100) nanoparticles (MCM@PEG-CO-DOX NPs), which could facilitate the NIR-responded CO-DOX combination therapy (Fig. 3(b)) [66]. Upon irradiation of \(808\;\mathrm{nm}\) laser, the photothermal effect of \(\mathrm{MCM}@\mathrm{PEG-CO-DOX}\) NPs can significant convert NIR to heat leading to the on-demand release of CO and DOX for GT enhanced chemotherapy [66]. Compared with the phototoxicity light of UV–vis light, visible light shows safety, convenience, and no-toxicity to normal tissues. Inspired by this, Diring et al. developed a novel CO donor of \(\mathrm{MnBr}(\mathrm{bpydc})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\) \((\mathrm{bpydc}~=~5,5^{\circ}\) -dicarboxylate- \({\cdot}2{,}2^{\circ}\) -bipyridine) that was loaded into a highly robust \(Z\mathrm{r}(\mathrm{IV})\) -based MOF to form \(\mathrm{MnBr}(\mathrm{bpydc})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}67\) [67]. Under irradiation of visible light, the \(\mathrm{MnBr}(\mathrm{bpydc})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}67\) NPs could efficiently release CO gas to induce apoptosis of HeLa cells (Fig. 3(c)).
It is known that the main advantages of US against light are the deeper penetration and minimal invasiveness that normally employed for the treatment of deep-seated cancer. An et al. prepared zeolite imidazole framework-8 loaded nitrosoglutathione (GSNO) and chlorin ee6 (Ce6), which was wrapped by homologous cancer cell membrane, named as \(\mathrm{GCZ}@\mathrm{M}\) (Fig. 3(d)) [68]. Specifically, \(\mathrm{GCZ}@\mathrm{M}\) could target tumor cell due to the cladding of homologous cancer cell membrane. The GSNO and Ce6 could release NO and generate ROS as triggered by US respectively, which would further react with each other to generate peroxynitrite (ONOO−) molecule and other reactive nitrogen species (RNS) that significantly induce tumor cell apoptosis [68].
## 2.2.2 Endogenous-responsive of MOF-based nanocarriers
The endogenous factors such as \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), pH, GSH, and enzyme, etc., play an important role in the adjustment of stimuli-responsive GRMs. As known to all, the overexpression of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in TME can be converted to \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) either by natural biological enzymes (e.g., catalase) or by inorganic enzyme mimetics like Au-, Pt-, Mn-, and
Fe-based nanomaterials [1]. For instance, Cheng and co-workers loaded CAT and photosensitizers Al (III) phthalocyanine chloride tetrasulfonic acid \(\left(\mathrm{AIPc}_{4}\mathrm{S}\right)\) in a tumor cell membrane-camouflaged zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF-8) (CAT-PS-ZIF@Mem), which catalyzed intracellular \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to produce \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) by CAT and enhanced the generation of toxic \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) for highly effective PDT [69]. Similarly, Feng et al. constructed a multifunctional ZIF-8 gated polydopamine (PDA) carrier that loaded CAT and photosensitizers to realize CAT-mediated \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) self-supply for efficient PDT (PDAs-MB-CAT-ZIF8) [70]. Moreover, Pt- and Au-based nanozymes could possess highly efficient activities similarly as natural nanozymes. Ren's group developed the nanoarchitectonics based on MOFs (PCN-224) containing Pt nanozymes and DOX (DOX@PCN-224/Pt), which not only could decompose \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to produce \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) for alleviating tumor hypoxia, but also enhance the ultrasound to generate more \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), as well as improve the effect of chemotherapy [71]. Similarly, He et al. reported an artificial enzyme (Au NPs) with upconversion NPs \((\mathrm{NaYF}_{4}@\mathrm{NaYb}_{0.92}\mathrm{F}_{4}:\mathrm{Er}_{0.08}@\mathrm{NaYF}_{4})\) zirconium/iron porphyrin MOF core–shell NPs (UMOFs@Au NPs) [72]. It was found that the constructed UMOFs@Au NPs have the glucose oxidase like catalytic activity that can effectively deplete glucose to generate \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) due to the ultrasmall Au NPs. Then the produced \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) is employed for the catalase-like reaction catalyzed to generate \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) for enhancing PDT by iron porphyrin MOF under NIR light irradiation [72].
Furthermore, a variety of CO-releasing molecules based on transition metal carbonyl compounds responsive to endogenous \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) have recently been reported [29, 73, 74]. Jin et al. loaded MnCO in a Ti-based MOF to realize an intratumoral \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\)-triggered CO release for GT (MnCO@Ti-MOF) [63]. Meanwhile, Guan and co-workers reported a Hf-NMOF NPs that loaded photosensitizer and CO prodrug MnCO to form UiO-type NMOF NPs [75], which can release CO gas from MnCO by the intracellular \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and effectively inhibit cellular energy metabolism.
Besides, pH triggers the degradation of pH-sensitive MOF nanocarriers to release therapeutic gas molecules that is another efficient strategy for tumor therapy. Xie's group design an \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\)-enhanced PDT synergistic CDT based on the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\)-Cu/ZIF-8@Ce6/ZIF-8@F127 NPs (Fig. 3(e)) [76]. After intravenous injection, the nanoparticles could accumulate into tumor site by the EPR effect and then release \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) molecules, Ce6, and \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) because of the acidic TME. The larger amount of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) would alleviate tumor hypoxia and further enhance the effect of PDT. Moreover, Gao et al. reported a bottom-up approach to prepare \(\mathrm{CaO}_{2}@\mathrm{DOX}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67\) NPs, which would be disassembled in acidic TME to rapidly release \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and DOX [77]. At the same time, the unprotected \(\mathrm{CaO}_{2}\) will react with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) to generate \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), which not only accelerates the \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) to produce ROS through a Fenton-like reaction, but also improves the efficacy of chemotherapy by alleviating the tumor hypoxic (Fig. 3(f)). Additionally, He and co-workers reported a new type of porphyrin-Fe-MOF that was integrated by the coordination between 5,10,15,20-tetrakis (4-pyridyl)-21H,23H-porphine (TPyP) and zero-valence Fe atom, then loaded with DOX to form DOX@Fe-MOF nanomedicine [78]. The obtained DOX@Fe-MOF can achieve simultaneously acid responsive release of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and DOX for hydrogen-chemotherapy. Moreover, the released \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) helps chemotherapeutic drugs to immunoactivate intratumoral M1 phenotype macrophages and consequently downregulate the expression of metastasis-associated matrix metalloproteinase 2 (MMP-2) to inhibit tumor-metastasis. Additionally, it can also reduce the expression of both P-gp protein and adenosine diphosphate (ATP) which benefits to sensitizing
chemotherapeutic drugs in MDR cancer cells.
## 2.3 Two-dimensional (2D)-based gas delivery nanoplatforms
2D-based nanomaterials exhibit good biocompatibility, larger specific surface area as well as easily surface modification, which always are used as carrier for drug delivery and play an important role in disease diagnosis and therapy. For instance, Liu et al. developed a PEGylated nanoscale graphene oxide (GO) as carrier to load with water insoluble aromatic molecule SN38 (a camptothecin analogue) and realize chemotherapy against HCT-116 cells in 2008 [79]. Subsequently, Zhang and co-workers found graphene oxide owned a huge drug carrying capacity of DOX as 4-fold than its own mass, attributed to the ultra-high specific surface that could firmly adsorb drugs via \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking and electrostatic interactions [80]. Moreover, carbon dots (C-dots) [81], \(\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) [82], black phosphorous [83], and zirconium boride nanosheet [84], etc., also show great potentials in clinical application of delivering GRMs.
## 2.3.1 Exogenous-responsive of 2D-based nanocarriers
Light stimuli-responsive is regarded to be a common route of controllable gas delivery due to the convenient and efficient. GO exhibits an excellent light absorption and photothermal conversion ability as well as larger specific surface area, which is quite suitable to be employed as drug/gas release carrier [85, 86]. For instance, an NIR-responsive nanoplatform by loading Mn-carbonyl CORMs within GO nanosheet was constructed by He et al., which was utilized both as a gas prodrug carrier and an NIR light energy converter to mediate the photochemical release of CO gas form CORMs [87]. As showed in Fig. 4(a), GO can absorb NIR light and transform photons into active electrons. The electrons were transferred from GO to coordinate Mn-carbonyl molecules, and contested the 3d orbitals of Mn with carbonyls, leading to the detachment of CO from Mn species [87]. Similarly, another NIR light responsive release nanoplatform (GO-BNN6) was constructed by self-assembly of GO nanosheets and BNN6 through the \(\pi{-}\pi\) attacking interaction [88]. NO could effectively release from GO-BNN6 due to the transform photons into active electrons from GO to BNN6 and then promote the decomposition of BNN6 under the irradiation of NIR light. Li and co-workers prepared a light triggered \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) -release platform (rGOPEI-DTC), which could convert NIR light into thermal energy and activate the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) donor (DTC) to generate \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), resulting in effective photothermal/gas therapy [89].
another NIR light responsive release nanoplatform (GO-BNN6) was constructed by self-assembly of GO nanosheets and BNN6 through the \(\pi{-}\pi\) attacking interaction [88]. NO could effectively release from GO-BNN6 due to the transform photons into active electrons from GO to BNN6 and then promote the decomposition of BNN6 under the irradiation of NIR light. Li and co-workers prepared a light triggered \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) -release platform (rGOPEI-DTC), which could convert NIR light into thermal energy and activate the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) donor (DTC) to generate \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) , resulting in effective photothermal/gas therapy [89].
Besides, C-dots, a new fluorescent nanoparticle with small size (less than \(10\ \mathrm{nm}\)) have excellent biocompatibility and cell membrane permeability as well as tunable surface functionalities, which have been widely used for the drug delivery and luminescence imaging [81, 90]. For example, Xu et al. reported a mitochondria-targeting and light-responsive release nanosystem coined as C-dot-triphenylphosphonium (TPP)-SNO, which conjugated NO donor (SNO) and TPP onto the C-dots to achieve NIR trigger NO and TPP release for efficient cancer therapy (Fig. 4(b)) [81].
Moreover, He and co-workers synthesized a hyaluronic acid (HA) modified 2D zirconium boride nanosheets (ZBN-HA) and consequently efficiently conjugated DOX and \(\beta\)-galactosyl-diazeniumdiolate (NO prodrug) on the surface of ZBN-HA to obtain ZBN-HA/DOX and ZBN-HA/NO nanomedicine (Fig. 4(c)) [84]. The photopyrolysis of borate ester causes HA detachment and ZBN aggregation, enabling NIR-controlled nanomedicine high intratumoral accumulation, and then achieves NIR-controlled drug and NO release for chemothermal/gasothermal therapies.
## 2.3.2 Endogenous-responsive of 2D-based nanocarriers
The overexpressed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in TME is a double-edged sword. Although \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) can breed cancer cells, it also can be employed as an endogenous stimulus to achieve drug-responsive release as well as ameliorate TME hypoxia for inhibiting tumor growth [82, 91–93]. Relieving tumor hypoxia not only reduces the
immunosuppressive of TME, but also improves the effect of tumor treatment, such as SDT, PDT, and RT, etc. Wei's group used a facile one-pot method to prepare an oxygen self-supply photosensitizer \(\left(\mathrm{Ru}-\mathrm{g}-\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\right)\) based on \(\left[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{2}\right]^{2+}\) coordinated to \(\mathrm{g}-\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) nanosheets by Ru–N bonding [82]. It is found that the Ru- \(\mathrm{g}-\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) nanosheets not only catalyze the decomposition of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to produce \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), but also enhance PDT (Fig. 4(d)). Notably, NO-mediated GT by disrupting the metabolism of cancer cell mitochondria is an effective anti-cancer strategy. For example, a NO-mediated phototherapeutic nanoplatform based on the poly L-arginine modified carbon dots-doped graphical carbon nitride nanomaterial (ArgCCN) was developed [92]. The ArgCCN NPs could generate holes to oxidize \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) into \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) under the irradiation of visible light, and subsequently react with L-arginine to produce NO to damage mitochondria and induce cancer cell apoptosis (Fig. 4(e)). Simultaneously, Liu and co-workers constructed a L-Arg based cancer nanodots (Arg-dots) nanomaterials, which can produce high level of NO gas in the tumor tissue by triggering the endogenous \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) (Fig. 4(f)) [93].
Additionally, Fan and co-workers fabricated a polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVP) modified 2D magnesium boride nanosheet (MBN@PVP) by an ultrasound-assisted chemical etching route, which could be served as a new type of acid-responsive hydrogen release prodrug [94]. When the MBN@PVP accumulated in TME, it could directly react with the \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\)to produce \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and significantly prolong the survival time of gastric tumor-bearing mice [94].
Liposomes were first discovered by scholar Bangham that phospholipids dispersed in water would form lipid bilayers [95]. Nowadays, liposome is well known as a closed vesicle with bilayer structure, which can efficiently encapsulate various drugs, including hydrophilic drugs in the inner cavity, and hydrophobic drugs in the middle of hydrophobic phospholipid bilayer [96]. Moreover, liposomes can reduce the toxicity of loaded drugs, and prolong the circulation time of drugs in vivo owning to their excellent biocompatibility and stability, which are always utilized as a good candidate of responsive drug release carrier for tumor therapy. Recently, some functional liposomes were reported to employ for encapsulating GRMs to realize stimuli-response release gaseous molecules under exogenous/endogenous triggers.
## 2.4.1 Exogenous-responsive of liposome-based nanocarriers
Liposomes are generally modified with targeting molecules for tumor-targeted delivery of GRMs to realize precision gas therapy [97, 98]. For example, Xu's group constructed a targeted delivery nanoplatform, thereinto, lipid was coated outside the UCNPs with a CO gaseous prodrug (CORM) in the lipid bilayer and a targeted molecules of folic acid (FA) was modified on the surface of lipid (lipid/UCNP/CORM/FA) [97]. This nanoplatform could efficiently target HCT116 tumor cells and convert NIR light to UV–vis light to active gas prodrug releasing CO (Fig. 5(a)). Furthermore, Wu et al. fabricated a biocompatible free radical nanogenerator by encapsulating an NIR II molecule (IR 1061), NO donor (BNN6), and alkyl radical initiator (AIPH) into a phase change liposome and further functionalized with a specific tumor
targeting molecule of DSPE-PEG-RGD (P(IR/BNN6/AIPH)@LipRGD) [98]. Upon NIR light irradiation, the nanogenerator could generate photothermal phase transition to trigger the release of BNN6 and AIPH, and subsequently decompose them to produce NO and alkyl radicals that efficiently induced tumor cell apoptosis (Fig. 5(b)).
## 2.4.2 Endogenous-responsive of liposome-based nanocarriers
Compared with the exogenous light-controlled gas release, the endogenous responsive modes (e.g., \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), pH, and GSH) are able to achieve accurate release of GRMs at the tumor site and ignore the limitation of penetration depth. For example, a DOX-loaded liposome holo-lactoferrin nanocomposites (Lf-Liposome-DOX) was prepared for tumor imaging guided radiochemotherapy [99]. As showed in Fig. 5(c), the Lf-Liposome-DOX NPs could target tumor cells owning to the transferrin receptor of holo-lactoferrin and then catalyze the conversion of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) for enhancing RT. Moreover, a pH-responsive NO release nanoparticle was constructed by dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine-based liposomes encapsulating of NO donors (Liposomal-DPTA/NO), which could trigger NO release in the acidic TME for against cancer cells (Fig. 5(d) [100]. Interestingly, Chen and co-workers reported a novel sequential strategy for improving pancreatic cancer therapy based on S-nitroso-N-acetylpenicillamine (SNAP) loaded liposomes (Lip-SNAP) [101]. The Lip-SNAP NPs could effectively inhibit the generation of dense stroma in pancreatic stellate tissue by suppressing the transforming growth factor beta1 (TGF- \(\beta1\) ) and its downstream profibrotic signal transduction, attributing to the effect of remarkably NO-release induced by the high level of GSH in TME (Fig. 5(e)).
## 3 GRMs delivery nanoplatform based on non-nanocarriers
Although nanocarriers exhibit a great application potential in drugloading and responsive drug-release, some disadvantages are also obvious, such as complex synthesis process, and long-term systemic toxicity. Therefore, direct preparation of organic/inorganic nanoparticles without additional nanocarriers for the stimuli-responsive gas release has received wide attention. Compared with traditional nanocarriers for GRMs delivery, nonnanocarrier nanoplatforms including inorganic-based nanoplatforms and organic-based nanoplatforms, have the following some advantages: (i) good cell permeability and tumor tissue EPR effect; (ii) almost having a \(100\%\) drug loading rate, benefiting to avoid the long-term systemic toxicity of inert additional carrier materials in vivo; (iii) facile preparation process, and favorable for mass production [102, 103]. In this section, the recent progresses on the non-nanocarriers nanoplatforms for GRMs delivery are introduced, which are also summarized in Table 2.
## 3.1 Inorganic-based functional nanoplatforms for GRMs delivery
The direct synthesis of inorganic-based nanoparticles as gas delivery nanoplatform that can directly be triggered to release gas molecular by exogenous/endogenous stimulus source and can be used in combination with other therapy strategies.
## 3.1.1 Exogenous-responsive of inorganic nanoplatforms
These GRMS can spatiotemporally responsive to exogenous stimuli-responsive, such as light, US, and X-rays. For example, Zhang et al. fabricated a NIR light triggered NO release nanoplatform by loading BNP (NO donor) on the surface of \(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}\)
nanoparticles (BNN- \(\cdot\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}\) NPs) to achieve PTT synergistic NO mediated GT (Fig. 6(a)) [104]. Due to the versatile performance of Pd hydride nanomaterials in self-catalytic hydrogenation, NIR light absorption, and photothermal conversion, He's group developed a cubic \(\mathrm{PdH}_{0.2}\) nanocrystals for photoacoustic imaging (PAI) guided hydrogenothermal therapy [105]. As a results, the \(\mathrm{PdH}_{0.2}\) exhibits an excellent capability of NIR-guided releasing of bio-reductive hydrogen, which could be synergistic with photothermal therapy for tumor treatment (Fig. 6(b)).
Interestingly, in order to achieve deep-tissue antitumor therapy, a soft X-ray activated and controlled NO release nanoplatform was developed to integrate \(\mathrm{ZnGa_{2}O_{4};M n}\) (ZGO:Mn) nanoparticles with RBS (NO donor) (ZGO:Mn-RBS NPs) [106]. Moreover, the ZGO:Mn-RBS NPs could be activated persistent luminescence and kept for long-lasting NO release after stopping the irradiation of X-ray. Furthermore, Yang's group fabricated a bismuth-based nano-theranostic agent via functionalized with SNO (NO donor) named Bi-SNO NPs [107]. Figure 6(c) shows that X-ray could break down the S–N bond and simultaneously trigger generating amount of NO, combining with the NIR-induced photothermal for remarkable synergistic antitumor.
## 3.1.2 Endogenous-responsive of inorganic nanoplatforms
The inherent characteristics of the TME (e.g., weak acidity, high level of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\)) are the prerequisites for activating gas prodrug molecules and realizing responsive gas release. Wang and coworkers reported an ultrasound/fluorescence imaging-guided nanoplatform based on chemotherapeutic drugs mitoxantrone (MTO) and \(\mathrm{MnCO}\) coined as MCMA NPs. The MCMA NPs could absorb NIR light and convert to high photothermal effect and then react with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to produce CO (Fig. 6(c)) [108]. Recently, our group reported polyvinyl pyrrolidone modified iron sulfide nanoparticles \(\mathrm{(Fe_{1-x}S-PV P\ N P s)}\) with high photothermal conversion efficiency, which could produce abundant hydroxyl radicals via Fenton reaction within the tumor region [23]. Importantly, under an acidic \(\mathrm{(pH=6.5)}\) condition within tumor environment, the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{1-x}S\) -PVP NPs can in situ produce \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) gas, which is evidenced to suppress the activity of enzyme cytochrome c oxidase (COX IV) in cancer cells, contributing to inhibit the growth of tumor (Fig. 6(e)) [23]. Notably, SulfoBiotics-HSip-1 (HSip-1) is a new fluorescent probe that can accurately detect \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), which is excited at \(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=491\ \mathrm{nm}\) when reacts with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and then emits green fluorescence at \(\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}=525\ \mathrm{nm}\) [109]. Zheng et al. found that unsaturated S atoms can capture protons in the TME and form \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) gas. Inspired by this, they constructed a gallic acid modified \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) nanosheets with high \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{II})\) loading \((\mathrm{MoS}_{2}@\mathrm{GA}-\mathrm{Fe})\) to realize CDT/GT (Fig. 6(f)) [110].
## 3.2 Organic-based functional nanoplatforms for GRMs delivery
The gas-responsive organic-based platforms were always synthesized by gas prodrug molecules via polymerized or self-assembled methods, further modification with targeted molecules, which could realize gas targeted delivery and controllable release.
## 3.2.1 Exogenous-responsive of organic nanoplatforms
Self-assembled organic nanoparticles exhibit an advantage of EPR effect to improve these nanoparticles further accumulation in specific tissues as tumor tissue and lymph nodes [111, 112]. As a paradigm, a \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) -flavone coordination polymer (NCu-FleCP) CO nano-prodrug was constructed through the coordination assembled strategy [112]. The NCu-FleCP nano-prodrug could effectively accumulate at the tumor site and react with high concentration of GSH to release free Fe and \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{I})\). When under
the irradiation of NIR laser, the C–C bond of Ele is cleavage, resulting in the release of larger amount of CO gas for GT (Fig. 7(a)). Similarly, Huang et al. developed a protoporphyrin (PpIX)-based polymer nanoplatform through a self-assembled strategy based on integrating PpIX and porphyrin modified Roussin's red-salt ester (PpRE) (PpRE@PEG-PpIX) [113]. As showed in Fig. 7(b), these nanoparticles revealed good photothermal performance and efficient NO release behavior under light irradiation, exhibiting a synergistic antitumor of PTT and GT. Furthermore, in order to break the tumor vascular barriers and enhance deep tumor tissue nanoparticles accumulation. Ren's group designed a SNO-HAS-PTX nanoparticles, which showed the US-trigger NO release behavior and then inhibited the tumor-associated platelets, resulting in destroying the tumor vascular barriers and promoting the
accumulation of SNO-HAS-PTX NPs [114].
3.2.2 Endogenous-responsive of organic nanoplatforms
Enhancing the tumor tissue penetration depth of nanoparticles and effectively targeting tumor cells is a prerequisite for efficient cancer therapy. Subsequently, in situ generation of therapeutic gas molecules by endogenous activation of nanoparticles is one of the effective means to achieve this strategy. On this ground, a NO-driven nanomotor was constructed and could move deep in the TME that induced the NO release to promote the movement behavior in regulating the infiltration and activity of immune cells [115]. This NO-driven nanomotor was self-assembled by heparin-folate-cy5.5/L-arginine (HFCA) and chemotherapeutic drug docetaxel (DTX), then loading immune checkpoint inhibitor anti-PD-1 (aPD1) to achieve NO guided chemotherapy/
immunotherapy (Fig. 7(c)) [115].
Recently, Ji's group used the acid-cleavable mitochondriapenetrating peptide PEG-(KDAKLAK)2CGKRK to self-assemble with \(\alpha\)-cyclodextrin (\(\alpha\)-CD)-based DOX and NO prodrug via like host–guest interaction to form A-CD-DOX-NO-DA NPs [116]. As shown in Fig. 7(d), the tumor extracellular acid can trigger the mitochondria-targeted A-CD-DOX-NO-DA NPs to release NO, which specifically induces mitochondria dysfunction through reducing mitochondrial membrane potential and downregulating ATP level, as well as inhibiting P-glycoprotein-related bioactivities.
In addition, it is reported that consuming endogenous GSH to release therapeutic gas, can not only reduce the intracellular GSH and increase the level of oxidative stress, but also improve the therapeutic effect of GT. For instance, Zhang et al. synthesized a galactose receptor targeted and GSH responsive NO generation nanoplatform, based on a self-assembled strategy through alkynylJSK (NO donor) and p(Gal-b-DPA-b-Az), the copolymer (referred to as p(GD-Az-JSK)), and DOX to form P(GD-AzJSK)/DOX NPs [117]. This nanoplatform could activated NOreleasing due to the responsive of intracellular GSH, exhibiting superior cytotoxicity to HepG2 cells with the synergistic DOX
chemotherapy (Fig. 7(e)). Moreover, Dai's group integrated an amphiphilic-conjugated polymer with a polysulfide-based \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) donor to develop a gas-modulated photothermal immunogenicity strategy (Pry-Ps@CP-PEG) [118]. When the nanoparticles uptake by the tumor cells, it could release \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) under the reaction with intracellular GSH, which would induce mitochondrial dysfunction to boost antitumor effect (Fig. 7(f)).
Furthermore, it is found that RNS are another kind of important free radical that can remarkable eliciting tumor cell apoptosis, including nitrogen dioxide \(\left(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right)\), nitrate anion \(\left(\mathrm{NO}^{-}\right)\), nitrite ion \(\left(\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}\right)\), and \(\mathrm{ONOO}^{-}\) [119]. To this point, Deng and coworkers developed a supramolecular pro-drug nanoassembly (TSPNAPt/NO), which was synthesized via self-assembly between host polymer PEG-b-PLys(Ad) and guest \(\beta\)-CD-based prodrugs [119]. After targeting liver cancer cells, \(\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{II})\) and NO were significantly released under an intracellular GSH environment, and then these \(\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{II})\) reacted with nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate oxidases \(\left(\mathrm{NOXs}\right)\) to generate \(\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\) and further activated the NO to produce highly toxic \(\mathrm{ONOO}^{-}\) against liver cancer (Fig. 7(g)). Additionally, low concentration NO was reported to improve angiogenesis appropriately and relieve tumor
hypoxia [120]. Inspired by the mentioned above, Tu et al. designed a micellar nanoplatform that was assembled by D-α-Tocopheryl polyethylene glycol 1000 succinate (NO donor) (TPGS-NO), which can trigger NO release by the intracellular GSH [120]. As shown in Fig. 7(h), the released NO could enhance tumor angiogenesis, alleviate tumor hypoxia and sensitize RT.
## 4 Conclusions and perspectives
In brief, compared with traditional therapy, as a promising green and specific therapeutic strategy, GT not only owns highly therapeutic efficacy, but also causes seldom systemic toxicity. More importantly, GT can be employed as adjuvant therapy to synergize with other modes of therapy (e.g., RT, chemotherapy, PDT, CDT, PTT, and SDT) to further enhance the anticancer effect. The previously reported gaseous molecules such as \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), NO, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), and CO, have been demonstrated to have the unique physiological functions and play a pivotal role in GT. Meanwhile, the recent development of theranostic nanomedicines provides a desirable strategy for the controlled release of the therapeutic gaseous molecules as well as stimuli-responsive GRMs, which can be activated by the exogenous/endogenous stimulates. In order to avoid the premature gas release, and rapid systemic clearance of GRMs, various multifunctional nanocarriers or non-nanocarriers nanoplatforms with good biocompatibility have been developed for efficient tumor delivery of GRMs. Despite some encouraging preclinical results so far, the multifunctional nanoplatforms for stimuli-responsive GRMs are still beset with challenges that need to be resolved, as listed in the following aspects.
## 4.1 Biosafety of GRMs delivery nanoplatforms
Although the GRMs delivery nanoplatforms show low systemic toxicity and good biocompatible at the suitable concentration, the nanoplatforms still have the long-term biosafety puzzle. The preliminary biosafety evaluations of these nanoplatforms are promising, but it will take longer to evaluate and optimize when it would enter the clinical translation, including biodistribution, biodegradation, metabolism, and hemo-compatibility, etc.
## 4.2 Biological mechanism of GT
The high therapeutic effect and biosafety that make GT own a good prospect for clinical translation. However, the exact biological mechanism of GT demands to be further explored. For example, high concentration of therapeutic gases can induce apoptosis of cancer cells, while low concentration can promote cancer cell growth. Thus, the accurate concentration threshold has not been determined at current stage. Moreover, it is also uncertain whether the same therapeutic gas is effective at various concentrations for different cancer cells types/stages. Therefore, the future researches should focus on the biological mechanism of GT, which will be beneficial to better control and optimise the promotion of therapeutic gas molecules in the further clinical applications.
## 4.3 Controllable gas release and precise delivery of nanoplatforms
The controllable gas release and precise delivery of nanoplatforms are an effective premise for achieving ideal GT. However, it is still in challenge to improve the sensitivity and controllability of responsive gas release based on TME stimuli since the difference between normal tissue and the TME is not obvious enough. Additionally, because of the limited depth of tissue penetration of light, light-triggered release of therapeutic gas is only effective for superficial tumor. Thus, it is urgent to develop much more nanoplatforms with high sensitivity to exogenous/endogenous stimuli to achieve efficient and controllable release of gaseous prodrugs when facing in the future clinical translation.
stimuli to achieve efficient and controllable release of gaseous prodrugs when facing in the future clinical translation.
## 4.4 Clinical translation of GRMs
Although various stimuli-responsive GRMs have been developed and show great advances in the field of nano medicine, only a few of these can be expected to be extended to clinical application. For instance, a clinical trial (NCT03818347) is currently underway to investigate the role of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in cancer recovery in China [121]. Additionally, another clinical study showed that inhalation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) was safe in patients with post-cardiac arrest syndrome in 2016, which exhibited a potential therapeutic application in other diseases [121, 122]. Nevertheless, before the formal clinical translation, the following issues urgently need to be considered, such as the dosage and administration, the specific biological mechanism and target, as well as the applicable population.
